{% extends "tool_layout" %}
{% import "icons" as icons %}

{# js/tools/pages/byte-size.mjs fills the rows. On a variant's page (e.g. MB
   to GB) it starts at 1 of its "from" unit, with its "to" unit picked out. #}
{% set groups = [
    ["decimal", "Decimal (1 KB = 1000 bytes)", [["B", "Bytes"], ["KB", "Kilobytes"], ["MB", "Megabytes"], ["GB", "Gigabytes"], ["TB", "Terabytes"], ["PB", "Petabytes"]]],
    ["binary", "Binary (1 KiB = 1024 bytes)", [["KiB", "Kibibytes"], ["MiB", "Mebibytes"], ["GiB", "Gibibytes"], ["TiB", "Tebibytes"], ["PiB", "Pebibytes"]]],
    ["bits", "Bits (8 bits = 1 byte)", [["bit", "Bits"], ["kbit", "Kilobits"], ["Mbit", "Megabits"], ["Gbit", "Gigabits"]]],
] %}

{% block tool %}
<section class="panel panel-pad tool-panel" id="byte-converter" data-from="{{ variant.from if variant else 'GB' }}"
    data-to="{{ variant.to if variant else 'GiB' }}" aria-label="Byte size converter">
    <div class="row g-2 align-items-start">
        <div class="col">
            <label for="bytes-in" class="visually-hidden">Size</label>
            <input id="bytes-in" class="form-control font-monospace bytes-in" type="text" inputmode="decimal"
                spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" value="1" placeholder="e.g. 1.5" aria-describedby="bytes-error">
            <div id="bytes-error" class="small text-warn mt-1" role="status"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="col-auto">
            <label for="bytes-unit" class="visually-hidden">Unit</label>
            <select id="bytes-unit" class="form-select">
                {% for group in groups %}
                <optgroup label="{{ group[1] }}">
                    {% for u in group[2] %}
                    <option value="{{ u[0] }}">{{ u[0] }} ({{ u[1] | lower }})</option>
                    {% endfor %}
                </optgroup>
                {% endfor %}
            </select>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p id="bytes-headline" class="bytes-headline mt-3 mb-0" role="status"></p>

    <div class="row g-4 mt-1">
        {% for group in groups %}
        <div class="col-lg-4">
            <h2 class="h6 fw-semibold mb-2">{{ group[1] }}</h2>
            <div class="d-flex flex-column gap-2">
                {% for u in group[2] %}
                <div class="bytes-row d-flex align-items-center gap-2" data-unit="{{ u[0] }}">
                    <label for="bytes-{{ u[0] }}" class="bytes-unit" title="{{ u[1] }}">{{ u[0] }}</label>
                    <input id="bytes-{{ u[0] }}" class="form-control form-control-sm font-monospace" type="text" readonly>
                    <button type="button" class="btn btn-quiet btn-sm" data-copy="bytes-{{ u[0] }}"
                        aria-label="Copy {{ u[1] | lower }}">{{ icons.icon("copy") }}</button>
                </div>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>
</section>
{% include "tool_variants" %}
{% endblock %}

{% block about %}
<h2 class="h4">KB or KiB: 1000 or 1024?</h2>
<p>There are two ways to count file sizes. <strong>Decimal</strong> units go up in thousands, like the rest of
    the metric system: 1 kilobyte (KB) is 1000 bytes and 1 megabyte (MB) is 1000 KB. <strong>Binary</strong> units
    go up in 1024s, a power of two that suits computer memory: 1 kibibyte (KiB) is 1024 bytes and 1 mebibyte (MiB)
    is 1024 KiB.</p>
<p>The gap grows with each step: a KiB is 2.4% bigger than a KB, but a TiB is almost 10% bigger than a TB. The
    trouble is that many programs show binary sizes with decimal names, so "1 GB" can mean either.</p>

<h2 class="h4">Why does my 1 TB drive show as 931 GB?</h2>
<p>Drive makers count in decimal, so a 1 TB drive holds 1,000,000,000,000 bytes. Windows counts in binary but
    labels the result "GB", so it shows those bytes as 931 GiB, written "931 GB". Nothing is missing. macOS and
    most phones count in decimal, and show the full 1 TB.</p>

<h2 class="h4">Bits and bytes</h2>
<p>A byte is 8 bits. Storage is measured in bytes (B), but network speeds are usually in bits per second
    (bit/s), so a 100 Mbit/s connection moves at most 12.5 MB of data a second. A lower-case <code>b</code>
    usually means bits and an upper-case <code>B</code> means bytes.</p>
{% endblock %}
